        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(memory-host-sdk): do not wipe archive on user-typed [cron: prefix",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`memory_search` silently drops all indexed content from a reset/deleted session archive when **any** user message in the transcript starts with `[cron:`. The user's prompt is treated as a server-set cron-run signal, which wipes everything collected so far and skips every remaining message — so unrelated remembered facts (vendor, budget, etc.) become unsearchable in the archive even though the live transcript stays intact. Reproducer and root-cause analysis: #98241.\n\nThis contradicts both the contract comment in `session-files.ts:111-113` (usage-counted archives \"must stay indexed so `memory_search` can surface hits on post-reset / post-delete history\") and the safety comment a few lines below the buggy block, which already warns that user-typed text can match these patterns and that a cross-message drop must not happen.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe text-based cross-message wipe in `buildSessionEntry` (the `!generatedByCronRun && allowArchiveContentCronClassification && isGeneratedCronPromptMessage(...)` branch) was unsafe: `DIRECT_CRON_PROMPT_RE = /^\\[cron:[^\\]]+\\]\\s*/` matches ordinary human text. A user pasting a cron log line, or asking \"[cron:daily-digest] why did my digest job fail last night?\", is enough to flip the whole archive to cron-generated and wipe all earlier + later content.\n\nThe structured `isCronRunGeneratedRecord` check earlier in the same function reads `sessionKey` provenance set by the gateway — user text cannot spoof that. The per-message cron-prompt drop still happens correctly inside `sanitizeSessionText` (so the actual cron-prefixed user prompt itself stays omitted), but it no longer cascades into a full-archive wipe.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Reset or deleted session archives containing a user message that starts with `[cron:` will now stay searchable in `memory_search` for everything except the cron-prefixed prompt itself (which is still dropped per-message by `sanitizeSessionText`).\n- No behavior change for archives without such a user message, and no change to the live `.jsonl` indexing.\n- No new config or migration surface; no compatibility shim — buggy branch is removed.\n\n## Evidence\n\nReproducer mirrors the issue's transcript exactly (4 messages: user `[cron:daily-digest] ...`, assistant reply, user remember request, assistant confirm). Added a regression test in `packages/memory-host-sdk/src/host/session-files.test.ts`:\n\n- `generatedByCronRun` is falsy\n- indexed content contains the assistant reply and the unrelated \"Please remember: my preferred vendor is Acme Robotics\" exchange\n- `lineMap` is `[2, 3, 4]` (the dropped `[cron:` user prompt becomes line 1, the rest are indexed)\n\nRemoved the prior test `keeps cron-run deleted archives opaque when the live session store entry is gone` because it enshrined the buggy behavior (asserting `content === \"\"` and `lineMap === []` for an archive triggered by user text). The structured-provenance path is still covered by the next test `keeps cron-run reset archives opaque when session metadata preserves the cron key`.\n\nLocal typecheck: `node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p tsconfig.core.json` → exit 0. Local oxlint on touched files: clean.\n\nFixes #98241\n",
        "branch": "fix/memory-host-cron-archive-98241",
